From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] mount-util: switch root with MS_MOVE before Linux 3.15

mount_switch_root_full() tucks the new root under the old one with
pivot_root(".", ".") and then runs umount2(".", MNT_DETACH), relying on that to
act on the top-most mount at the cwd, the old root.

umount2() resolves its path that way only since Linux 3.15, which brought
user_path_mountpoint_at(). On the LuneOS Halium machines with 3.4 and 3.10
kernels umount2(".") resolves to the mount the cwd itself is on, so it detaches
the new root and leaves the process in a mount that belongs to no namespace. The
next mount() on it faults in the 3.4 kernel:

  mount(NULL, "/", NULL, MS_REC|MS_SHARED, NULL) -> killed by SIGSEGV
  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 00000014
  PC is at do_mount+0x52c/0xb24

and every unit that sets up a mount namespace (ProtectSystem=, PrivateTmp=, ...)
dies, including systemd-udevd, systemd-logind and systemd-userdbd. On the 3.10
Nexus 5X, whose umount has the same lookup, the same units exit with
226/NAMESPACE without saying why; this is the likely cause, not a proven one.

Reproduced outside systemd, in a forked child on a booted 3.4 device: setting
the propagation of "." works right after pivot_root() and crashes only after
umount2(".", MNT_DETACH). The MS_MOVE and chroot() sequence the function already
has as its fallback does the switch without crashing, and a following
propagation change succeeds.

Take that fallback when the running kernel is older than 3.15. Newer kernels
keep pivot_root().

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [systemd's minimum kernel is much newer than 3.15]
---
--- a/src/shared/mount-util.c
+++ b/src/shared/mount-util.c
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 #include <sys/mount.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <sys/statvfs.h>
+#include <sys/utsname.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <linux/loop.h>
 #if WANT_LINUX_FS_H
@@ -517,6 +518,20 @@
         if (fchdir(fd_newroot) < 0)
                 return log_debug_errno(errno, "Failed to chdir into new rootfs '%s': %m", path);
 
+        /* mount_switch_root_pivot() relies on umount2(".", MNT_DETACH) acting on the top-most mount at the
+         * cwd. umount2() resolves its path that way only since Linux 3.15 (user_path_mountpoint_at()); an
+         * older kernel resolves "." to the mount the cwd itself is on, so it detaches the new root instead
+         * of the stacked old one, and the next mount() on it fails or, on 3.4, oopses. MS_MOVE plus chroot()
+         * behaves the same on every kernel, so take it there. */
+        if (!force_ms_move) {
+                struct utsname u;
+                unsigned major, minor;
+
+                if (uname(&u) >= 0 && sscanf(u.release, "%u.%u", &major, &minor) == 2 &&
+                    (major < 3 || (major == 3 && minor < 15)))
+                        force_ms_move = true;
+        }
+
         /* Make this a NOP if we are supposed to switch to our current root fs. After all, both pivot_root()
          * and MS_MOVE don't like that. */
         if (!is_current_root) {
